s onto  roadways over one thousand soldiers are  being deployed to the disaster stricken  area to help with the rescue efforts  meet britain's prime minister ahead can  liz trust shake off the baggage of her  old boss  weeks after the fbi raided donald  trump's mar-a-lago mansion seizing many  highly classified documents in his  possession  a trump-appointed federal judge has  granted his request for a special master  to review the materials the move will  delay the department of justice's probe  of the former u.s president on possible  obstruction of justice and violations of  the espionage act with the judge also  blocking the doj's review of sensitive  files until the watchdog's examination  is finished both trump's legal team and  the doj must now jointly submit a list  of candidates by friday and outlined  their duties and restrictions federal  prosecutors are expected to appeal the  decision  tomorrow newly minted uk conservative  leader liz truss will become the  country's new prime minister replacing  boris johnson this is the third change  of leader for the ruling conservatives  in just six years trust has served in a  number of top jobs in the british  cabinet and even spent a year for  childhood here in canada but as redmond  shannon reports she's facing a long list  of political problems and potential  crises  81 000 in a nation no stranger to  political drama this outcome came as  little shock liz truss is elected as the  leader of the conservative and unionist  party but the margin of liz truss's win  over former british finance minister  rishi sunak was tighter than some had  predicted 57 of conservative party  members to 43. i know  that we will deliver we will deliver and  we will deliver compared with boris  johnson trust can seem short on bombast  and charisma perhaps both a blessing and  a curse following the non-stop  controversy of boris's reign my first  priority is reducing taxes the outgoing  foreign secretary wooed the membership  with some typically tory promises but  these are anything but typical times war  in ukraine high inflation energy bills  surging industrial strikes and the still  unresolved headache of brexit truss  originally opposed the uk leaving the eu  but like predecessor theresa may she  flipped after the 2016 referendum a  young truss spent grade 7 in burnaby  british columbia calling it a year that  changed her outlook on life former  classmate and current teacher at the  same school brenda montagno recalls how  truss fit right in totally remember her  as elizabeth but no i have not been  tracking her so it was pretty exciting  and have certainly been googling her  since and very exciting to see the news  today years before becoming a  conservative a young trust addressed the  party convention of the centrist liberal  democrats arguing to abolish the  monarchy  we do not  believe that people should be born to  rule trust has since changed her  position on the monarchy too it's just  as well on tuesday morning she and  johnson fly to scotland to meet the  queen for trusses swearing in as leader  it's then back here to london where we  expect to hear more about the vision and  the policies of the uk's latest prime  minister redmond shannon global news  london  inflation and the war in ukraine are  also on the minds of eu leaders as  financial markets opened with the euro  hitting a 20-year low the fallen values  directly related to the rise in gas  prices following russia's decision to  stop the flow of natural gas through the  north stream one pipeline financial  analysts say they expect the euro to  drop even more the crisis is having a  ripple effect outside of europe too with  markets in australia china and the uk  also taking a hit  the separation nuclear power plant at  the center of tensions between russia  and ukraine has been taken offline once  again according to the plant's operator  shelling knocked out power to the last  working reactor on the site this follows  days of 